      <ArticleContact line="Comparing two homes with different dues? Contact me." />

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">9. What Buyers Should Review Before Making an Offer</h2>
      <p>Before committing to a property, buyers should request and review the relevant information for that specific home.</p>
      <p><strong>Property tax information</strong></p>
      <p>Obtain the most recent available tax bill and identify the base property tax, voter-approved charges, special assessments, and any Mello-Roos or CFD entries.</p>
      <p><strong>HOA documents</strong></p>
      <p>Where applicable, review the association’s governing documents, current budget, financial statements, reserve information, meeting minutes, insurance information, and assessment history.</p>
      <p><strong>Special assessments</strong></p>
      <p>Determine whether any assessments are currently due, approved, proposed, or under discussion. Ask how they are allocated and whether any unpaid balance will be addressed during escrow.</p>
      <p><strong>Community amenities and responsibilities</strong></p>
      <p>Confirm which amenities are included, which facilities are maintained by the association, and whether there are separate charges for particular services.</p>
      <p><strong>Future costs</strong></p>
      <p>Look for indications of deferred maintenance, inadequate reserves, upcoming projects, or potential increases in dues.</p>
      <p>Buyers should review the documents within the applicable contingency and disclosure periods and consult qualified professionals when necessary.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">10. What Current Homeowners Should Know</h2>
      <p>These expenses are not only important to buyers. Existing homeowners should periodically review their property tax bills and HOA communications.</p>
      <p>Property tax bills can change as assessed values, voter-approved charges, and special assessments change. HOA dues may also increase as operating expenses, insurance premiums, maintenance costs, and reserve requirements evolve.</p>
      <p>Homeowners should keep copies of tax bills, association notices, budgets, and assessment information. If a charge is unclear, the appropriate county office, district, or association should be contacted directly.</p>
      <p>In Riverside County, questions about special assessments should be directed to the district or agency identified on the tax bill. Questions about property tax assessments and billing should be directed to the appropriate county office.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">11. Temecula Neighborhoods: Compare the Property, Not Just the Community Name</h2>
      <p>Temecula offers a wide range of housing options, including established neighborhoods, newer master-planned communities, gated developments, golf-course communities, and properties in Wine Country.</p>
      <p>Each property can have its own combination of tax obligations, HOA responsibilities, and maintenance costs.</p>
      <p>Even within a familiar neighborhood, buyers should verify the details of the exact home. Tax assessments can differ by parcel, and HOA responsibilities may vary depending on the type of property and association structure.</p>
      <p>This is particularly important when comparing a newer home with an established resale property. The purchase price alone does not reveal the complete cost of ownership.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">12. The Bottom Line: Know the Full Cost Before You Buy</h2>
      <p>Property taxes, HOA dues, and Mello-Roos assessments are not necessarily reasons to avoid a particular home or neighborhood. They are simply part of understanding what ownership will cost.</p>
      <p>For some buyers, a community with extensive amenities and an HOA may offer valuable convenience and lifestyle benefits. Others may prefer a property with fewer shared obligations. A home with Mello-Roos may still be the right choice if its location, features, and overall value fit the buyer’s priorities.</p>
      <p>The most important step is to understand the actual numbers before making a decision. Reviewing the tax bill, association documents, and applicable special assessments can help buyers compare properties accurately and plan with confidence.</p>
      <p>A knowledgeable real estate professional can help identify the documents to review and explain how these expenses fit into the overall purchase process. For tax, legal, lending, and HOA-governance questions, buyers should also consult the appropriate qualified professionals and public agencies.</p>
